# Lesson 7: Claude Code inside Neovim

claudecode.nvim connects the `claude` CLI to Neovim, like the VS Code
extension: Claude sees the file you're in and what you've selected, and its
edits open as diffs in Neovim for you to accept or reject. It runs on your
normal Claude Code account.

Open the practice project: `:Practice`{vim}  (`]t`{normal} / `[t`{normal} switch tabs)

# 7.1 Open and move around

  `<Space>Ac`{normal}   open / close Claude in a split on the right
  `<Space>Af`{normal}   jump between your code and Claude
  `<C-h>`{normal} / `<C-l>`{normal}   move left / right between them (works while typing in Claude too)
  `<Space>Ar`{normal}   resume a previous conversation (pick one)
  `<Space>AC`{normal}   continue the most recent conversation
  `<Space>Am`{normal}   choose the model

Claude's split is a terminal. To scroll its output: `<C-\>`{normal} `<C-n>`{normal}, then `k`{normal}/`j`{normal};
`i`{normal} goes back to typing.

 1. In the practice tab, open src/cart.ts and press `<Space>Ac`{normal}.
 2. Ask Claude: "What does this file do?" It already knows which file you're in.
 3. `<C-h>`{normal} back to the code, `<C-l>`{normal} back to Claude.

# 7.2 Give Claude exactly the code you mean

  `<Space>Ab`{normal}            add the whole current file to the conversation
  select, then `<Space>As`{normal}   send the selection (visual mode)

Selecting is the fastest way to say "this bit":
 1. In cart.ts, go to the `add(item: CartItem)` method. `V`{normal} then `j`{normal} `j`{normal} to select
    its three lines, then `<Space>As`{normal}.
 2. In Claude, ask: "Do the TODO above this: merge lines with the same sku
    instead of adding duplicates."

# 7.3 Review Claude's edits as diffs

When Claude wants to change a file, Neovim opens the change as a diff:
the original next to the proposal.

  `<Space>Aa`{normal}   accept the diff (the change is written)
  `<Space>Ad`{normal}   reject it (Claude is told, and you can explain why)

 1. When the cart.ts diff appears, read it. Use `]c`{normal} / `[c`{normal} to jump between
    the changed blocks in a diff.
 2. If it looks right, `<Space>Aa`{normal}. If not, `<Space>Ad`{normal} and tell Claude what
    to change.
 3. Then check its work with lesson 5: `]d`{normal} for new errors, and lesson 6:
    `]g`{normal} / `<Space>gp`{normal} to see exactly what changed since the last commit.

# 7.4 A good working loop

  1. Navigate to the code (`<Space>ff`, `gd`, `grr`).
  2. Select the part you mean (`V` for lines, or `S` to select a whole
     function or block with flash Treesitter) and `<Space>As`.
  3. Ask; review the diff; `<Space>Aa` or `<Space>Ad`.
  4. Verify: `]d` for errors, `<Space>Tf` for tests, `<Space>gp` for the change.
  5. Commit with `<Space>gg`.

`S`{normal} (flash Treesitter) is the quickest way to select a whole function or
block before sending it: press `S`{normal}, then the label on the block you want.

# Recap

  open  `<Space>Ac`   focus  `<Space>Af`   move  `<C-h>` `<C-l>`
  send selection  `<Space>As`   add file  `<Space>Ab`
  accept  `<Space>Aa`   reject  `<Space>Ad`   resume  `<Space>Ar`   continue  `<Space>AC`

That's the series. Run any lesson again with `:Tutor my-`{vim} and `<Tab>`{normal}.
Keep `:Hardtime report`{vim} and `tips nvim` going, and in a couple of weeks
none of this will need thinking about.
